# L §4.4 (tally 2.1.8 R13, X1, X2; K1): a plain line may answer with a window (L §5.18: an imprecise date prints as
# its window). A computed interval carries the names of the unknown days it was computed from, and windows each takes
# them: the business day after Friday 2026-05-29 is 06-01 or 06-02 as the calendar's added entry falls (q). The
# entry of the row governing December, which q never reads, is in no head. The same lines as 2.1.7's refcore.
=== m.tally
model k1
  tally 2
  on 2026-03-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source card "invented"

input cal: calendar

ask qp: add_business(2026-05-29, 1, cal)
ask q: add_business(2026-05-29, 1, cal) windows each

version c1
  cite card
  governs ..2026-11-30
  known 2026-01-01
  cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], added [window(2026-06-01, 2026-06-02)]) "c"

version c2
  cite card
  governs 2026-12-01..
  known 2026-01-01
  cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], added [window(2026-12-01, 2026-12-02)]) "c"
=== expect
q [added window(2026-06-01, 2026-06-02) = 2026-06-01]: 2026-06-02
  cut 2026-03-01
q [added window(2026-06-01, 2026-06-02) = 2026-06-02]: 2026-06-01
  cut 2026-03-01
qp: window(2026-06-01, 2026-06-02)
  cut 2026-03-01
